didate, or clear evidence that A\u2019s resistance to new ideas or show\u2011off behavior compromises safety or crew performance.\nMETADATA_JSON:\n{\"agent\": \"agent_2\", \"vote\": \"Candidate A\"}", "thoughts": "We have info about all candidates. Need to evaluate holistically. Summarize strengths/weaknesses.\n\nCandidate A: strengths: anticipate dangerous situations, see complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities. Weaknesses: regarded as a show-off, not open to new ideas. Also maybe show-off could affect teamwork. Not open to new ideas hurts adaptability.\n\nCandidate B: strengths: very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills. Weaknesses: relatively weak memory for numbers, makes nasty remarks about colleagues (bad teamwork), maybe uncooperative.\n\nCandidate C: strengths: can make correct decisions quickly, keeps calm in crisis, understands complicated technology. Weaknesses: difficulty communicating ideas, regarded as egocentric, not willing to further education.\n\nCandidate D: strengths: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously. Weaknesses: regarded as a know-it-all, hot temper.\n\nWe need to weigh for long-distance pilot: reliability, technical competence, calm under pressure, decision-making, teamwork/communication, professional development.\n\nCandidate A: strong technical (spatial vision, anticipate danger, complex connections) and leadership, but show-off and not open to new ideas may affect crew cooperation and adaptability. No explicit teamwork evidence.\n\nCandidate B: good stress handling, conscientious, weather assessment, computer skills (technical), but memory weakness for numbers (could affect navigation), nasty remarks (bad teamwork). So teamwork is a problem.\n\nCandidate C: quick correct decisions, calm in crisis, tech understanding (good), but communication difficulty, egocentric, not willing to further education (professional development). Communication is key for crew.\n\nCandidate D: problem solving, concentration, responsibility, handles unexpected events, but hot temper and know-it-all attitude (teamwork issues). No explicit technical flight skill but problem solving and concentration are important.\n\nWe need to pick best overall. Likely Candidate A has strong technical and leadership, but lack of openness may be mitigated? Candidate D has strong problem solving and responsibility but hot temper is serious for crew. Candidate C has calmness and decision speed but poor communication. Candidate B has conscientiousness and stress handling but nasty remarks and memory issues.\n\nProbably Candidate A is best despite show-off, because technical and leadership are crucial, and show-off may be less severe than hot temper or egocentric. Also not open to new ideas could be mitigated with training. So I will provisionally favor Candidate A.\n\nUncertainties: lack of explicit teamwork evidence for A, unknown about D's technical competence, unknown about B's memory impact, unknown about C's willingness to improve.\n\nWhat would change: evidence of strong teamwork for another candidate, or severe safety concerns for A.\n\nNow produce output."}
