rship, but interpersonal issues may affect crew. No explicit reliability but leadership suggests. Not open to new ideas could affect professional development.\n\nCandidate B: good reliability (conscientious), stress handling, weather assessment, but memory weakness and poor teamwork.\n\nCandidate C: reliability (100% reliable), positive atmosphere, good decision-making, calm, technical understanding, but communication difficulty and egocentric, no desire for further education.\n\nCandidate D: responsibility, problem solving, concentration, adaptability, but temperament issues (hot temper, know-it-all) and possibly loner.\n\nWe need to choose provisional preference. Which candidate best balances? Candidate C has explicit reliability and positive crew atmosphere, plus calmness and decision-making, but communication difficulty may be mitigated? Egocentric and unwilling to further education are concerns for long-term. However, teamwork strengths are strong.\n\nCandidate A has strong technical but lacks teamwork evidence and has show-off attitude.\n\nCandidate B has teamwork issues.\n\nCandidate D has temperament issues.\n\nThus Candidate C may be best overall due to reliability, crew atmosphere, calmness, decision-making, technical competence, despite communication issues. But communication is crucial for crew coordination. However, the positive atmosphere may offset some.\n\nWe need to decide. I think Candidate C is strong candidate. The group currently leaning to A per agent_2, but we have new info about C's teamwork strengths. We should weigh.\n\nGiven long-distance flights require crew coordination, reliability, calmness, decision-making, and ability to maintain morale. Candidate C's positive atmosphere and reliability are key. Communication difficulty is a drawback but maybe less severe than show-off or hot temper.\n\nThus I will provisionally favor Candidate C.\n\nWe need to state confidence, uncertainties: communication difficulty, egocentric attitude, lack of pr